INT. FLOODED PUMP HOUSE - RICE TERRACES - DAWN

Grey light leaks through a tin roof pocked with shrapnel holes. Knee-deep brown water slops against concrete walls. SERGEANT HADIZA BELLO, forties, mud to the elbows, crouches on a pump housing with a radio handset wedged against her shoulder, while CORPORAL TOMEK WRONA, a lanky twenty-two-year-old with a bandaged ear, wades in from the doorway and sets a dented ammo can on the ledge beside her. She doesn't look at him. He opens the can, counts what's left, and shuts it again, and behind them PRIVATE LI WEI, nineteen, thin as a rake, sits against the far wall with his rifle across his knees, staring at the water as if it might hand him an answer.

BELLO
(into handset)
Say again, Control. Say again.

Static. She lowers the handset and lets it hang by its cord.

WRONA
Anything?

BELLO
They're calling it off.

WRONA
Off.

BELLO
The push. Guns have stopped. Whatever they were going to do up the hill, they're not doing it.

Li Wei looks up. He tries to speak, coughs, and tries again.

LI WEI
So we go home?

BELLO
We go back to the road. That's not the same thing.

She slides off the housing into the water and he grimaces at the cold of it, then steadies himself on the wall while Wrona reaches for the can and hauls it onto his shoulder. They're all slow now, all of them moving like men who have been told to stop running and haven't yet believed it.

WRONA
Captain's still up there.

BELLO
I know where the Captain is.

A VOICE crackles from the handset, small and tinny.

CAPTAIN DUARTE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Bello. Bello, it's Duarte. I'm coming down. Hold the pump house.

Bello lifts the handset. She stares at it a moment before answering, and Wrona watches her face for the thing she won't say.

BELLO
(into handset)
Holding, sir.

She hangs it back on its hook. Li Wei lets out a breath that turns into a laugh and then into something quieter. Nobody tells him to stop.

LI WEI
I thought we were done.

BELLO
We are done. Get some water in you.

She wades to the door and leans on the frame, looking out at the terraces going silver in the light. Wrona joins her, and for a while neither of them says anything at all.

WRONA
It's quiet.

BELLO
Don't say that out loud.
